// ============================================================
// FILE: 01_supply_chain_kpi_measures.dax
// PROJECT: Automotive Supply Chain Analytics - Power BI
// DESCRIPTION: Comprehensive DAX measure library organized by category.
//              All measures reference the data model defined in
//              02_power_bi_data_model.md.
// AUTHOR: Supply Chain Analytics Team
// LAST UPDATED: 2026-05-15
// ============================================================
//
// NAMING CONVENTION:
//   [Category Prefix] Measure Name
//   e.g., [SC] On-Time Delivery %   (SC = Supply Chain)
//         [MFG] Plant Utilization %  (MFG = Manufacturing)
//         [SLS] Forecast Accuracy %  (SLS = Sales)
//         [TI] Revenue YTD           (TI = Time Intelligence)
//         [ALT] Supplier Risk Status (ALT = Alert / Conditional)
//
// HOW TO IMPORT:
//   In Power BI Desktop, open the DAX editor for a new measure,
//   paste each block below, and confirm. Alternatively, use
//   Tabular Editor 2/3 to bulk-import all measures at once.
// ============================================================


// ============================================================
// SECTION 1: SUPPLY CHAIN KPIs
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 1: On-Time Delivery %
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   The percentage of shipments that arrived at or before their
//   scheduled delivery date (i.e., delay_days <= 0).
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   On-Time Delivery (OTD) is the primary metric for evaluating
//   carrier and supplier performance. A low OTD directly causes
//   production line stoppages, customer dissatisfaction, and
//   expediting costs.
//
// BUSINESS INTERPRETATION:
//   > 95%  = Industry best practice (Tier 1 Automotive)
//   90-95% = Acceptable; investigate recurring late suppliers
//   < 90%  = Critical; escalate to supplier management
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Place as a KPI card on Executive Overview (Dashboard 1).
//   Use supplier slicer on Dashboard 2 to isolate underperformers.
//   Set conditional formatting: green >= 95, amber 90-94, red < 90.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[SC] On-Time Delivery % =
DIVIDE(
    CALCULATE(
        COUNTROWS( fact_shipments ),
        fact_shipments[delay_days] <= 0
    ),
    COUNTROWS( fact_shipments ),
    0
) * 100


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 2: Inventory Turnover
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   How many times the company sells through its average inventory
//   during a period. Formula: COGS / Average Inventory Value.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   High turnover signals efficient inventory management and
//   reduces holding costs. Very high turnover may indicate
//   stockout risk. Very low turnover indicates overstock or
//   slow-moving parts (obsolescence risk in automotive).
//
// BUSINESS INTERPRETATION:
//   Automotive OEM benchmark: 12-20x per year (monthly target ~1.5x).
//   < 8x  = Overstocked / potential write-off risk
//   8-12x = Monitor; optimize reorder points
//   > 20x = Risk of stockout; review safety stock
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Trend line on Inventory Dashboard (Dashboard 3).
//   Slice by product category (raw material vs WIP vs finished).
//   Drill through to warehouse-level detail.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[SC] Inventory Turnover =
VAR COGS =
    SUMX(
        fact_sales,
        fact_sales[units_sold] * RELATED( dim_vehicle[unit_cost] )
    )
VAR AvgInventoryValue =
    AVERAGEX(
        VALUES( dim_date[date] ),
        CALCULATE( SUM( fact_inventory[inventory_value] ) )
    )
RETURN
    DIVIDE( COGS, AvgInventoryValue, 0 )


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 3: Fill Rate %
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   The percentage of ordered units that were actually delivered.
//   Fill Rate % = Units Delivered / Units Ordered * 100.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   A fill rate below 100% means customers or production lines
//   received less than requested, potentially halting assembly.
//   In automotive, even a 1% fill rate shortfall can shut down
//   a production line costing $50,000+ per hour.
//
// BUSINESS INTERPRETATION:
//   >= 98% = Target (Tier 1 automotive standard)
//   95-98% = Acceptable with root-cause review
//   < 95%  = Immediate escalation required
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Use alongside Stockout Rate on Dashboard 3.
//   Break down by supplier on Dashboard 2 to find chronic underdelivery.
//   Tooltip on shipment map showing fill rate by region.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[SC] Fill Rate % =
DIVIDE(
    SUM( fact_shipments[units_delivered] ),
    SUM( fact_shipments[units_ordered] ),
    0
) * 100


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 4: Average Lead Time
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   The arithmetic mean of lead_time_days across all shipments
//   in the current filter context (supplier, region, date range).
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   Lead time drives safety stock calculations and production
//   scheduling. Increasing lead times signal supplier capacity
//   constraints or logistics disruptions before they become crises.
//
// BUSINESS INTERPRETATION:
//   Benchmark varies by part category and sourcing geography.
//   Domestic:       3-7 days target
//   International: 14-30 days target
//   Monitor week-over-week trend for early warning.
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Trend line on Supplier Performance dashboard (Dashboard 2).
//   Compare against contract SLA lead time (dim_supplier[sla_lead_time_days]).
//   Alert when actual > SLA by more than 20%.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[SC] Average Lead Time =
AVERAGE( fact_shipments[lead_time_days] )


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 5: Supplier Reliability Score
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   The average of supplier_reliability_score from dim_supplier,
//   weighted by shipment count so high-volume suppliers have
//   proportional influence on the aggregate.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   A composite reliability score encapsulates on-time delivery,
//   quality, and communication responsiveness into a single KPI
//   for executive decision-making on supplier qualification.
//
// BUSINESS INTERPRETATION:
//   Score is on a 0-100 scale.
//   >= 85 = Preferred supplier
//   70-84 = Approved with improvement plan
//   < 70  = At-risk; dual-source or replace
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Scatter plot on Dashboard 2: X = Reliability Score, Y = Volume.
//   Color by risk tier. Size bubble by spend.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[SC] Supplier Reliability Score =
AVERAGEX(
    VALUES( dim_supplier[supplier_id] ),
    CALCULATE(
        AVERAGE( dim_supplier[reliability_score] )
    )
)


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 6: Transportation Cost Ratio
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   Total transportation spend as a percentage of total revenue.
//   Helps quantify logistics efficiency relative to business scale.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   Transportation is typically 5-10% of revenue in automotive
//   supply chains. Increases may indicate carrier rate hikes,
//   route inefficiencies, or emergency air freight usage.
//
// BUSINESS INTERPRETATION:
//   < 5%   = Excellent; review for under-investment risk
//   5-8%   = Industry standard
//   8-10%  = Elevated; review modal mix (road vs rail vs air)
//   > 10%  = Critical; renegotiate contracts, optimize routing
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   KPI card on Dashboard 4 (Logistics).
//   Trend by month to spot seasonal spikes.
//   Break down by transport mode for modal analysis.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[SC] Transportation Cost Ratio =
DIVIDE(
    SUM( fact_shipments[transportation_cost] ),
    SUM( fact_sales[revenue] ),
    0
) * 100


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 7: Perfect Order Rate
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   Percentage of orders that were: (1) on time (delay_days <= 0),
//   (2) defect-free (defect_units = 0), AND (3) correct quantity
//   (units_delivered = units_ordered). All three conditions must
//   be true simultaneously.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   Perfect Order Rate is the gold standard of supply chain
//   performance. It captures the end-to-end customer experience
//   in a single number. A 95% OTD and 98% fill rate combined
//   still yields a Perfect Order Rate below 93%.
//
// BUSINESS INTERPRETATION:
//   > 90%  = World class
//   80-90% = Good; identify the dominant failure mode
//   < 80%  = Systemic issues; cross-functional review required
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Executive KPI card (Dashboard 1).
//   Waterfall chart decomposing imperfect orders by failure reason.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[SC] Perfect Order Rate =
VAR TotalOrders = COUNTROWS( fact_shipments )
VAR PerfectOrders =
    CALCULATE(
        COUNTROWS( fact_shipments ),
        fact_shipments[delay_days] <= 0,
        fact_shipments[defect_units] = 0,
        fact_shipments[units_delivered] = fact_shipments[units_ordered]
    )
RETURN
    DIVIDE( PerfectOrders, TotalOrders, 0 ) * 100


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 8: Stockout Rate
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   The proportion of warehouse-product combinations where current
//   inventory falls below the defined safety stock level.
//   Calculated as: warehouses below safety stock / total warehouse rows.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   Stockouts in automotive stop production lines. Even a 1%
//   stockout rate can mean dozens of critical parts are dangerously
//   low. This metric drives replenishment priority decisions.
//
// BUSINESS INTERPRETATION:
//   0%     = Ideal (all locations above safety stock)
//   1-3%   = Monitor; review replenishment triggers
//   > 3%   = Critical; initiate emergency procurement
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Heatmap by warehouse on Dashboard 3.
//   Alert badge when stockout rate > 3%.
//   Drill through to see which SKUs and warehouses are affected.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[SC] Stockout Rate =
DIVIDE(
    CALCULATE(
        COUNTROWS( fact_inventory ),
        fact_inventory[quantity_on_hand] < fact_inventory[safety_stock_level]
    ),
    COUNTROWS( fact_inventory ),
    0
) * 100


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 9: Days Inventory Outstanding (DIO)
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   How many days, on average, inventory sits before being used
//   or sold. DIO = 365 / Inventory Turnover.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   DIO is a component of the Cash Conversion Cycle. High DIO
//   ties up working capital and increases obsolescence risk,
//   which is especially costly for automotive parts with
//   model-year lifecycle constraints.
//
// BUSINESS INTERPRETATION:
//   < 20 days = Lean / risk of stockout
//   20-45 days = Typical automotive target
//   > 60 days = Overstocked; review procurement strategy
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   KPI card on Dashboard 3. Trend over time.
//   Compare against Days Payable Outstanding for working capital view.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[SC] Days Inventory Outstanding =
VAR InvTurnover = [SC] Inventory Turnover
RETURN
    IF(
        InvTurnover = 0,
        BLANK(),
        DIVIDE( 365, InvTurnover, BLANK() )
    )


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 10: Supplier Risk Index
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   A weighted composite risk score per supplier combining:
//     - financial_risk_score    (weight: 40%)
//     - geopolitical_risk_score (weight: 30%)
//     - quality_risk_score      (weight: 20%)
//     - lead_time_risk_score    (weight: 10%)
//   Higher score = higher risk (scale 0-100).
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   Single-source automotive suppliers represent existential risk.
//   A composite index allows procurement teams to rank suppliers
//   for dual-sourcing investment and contingency planning.
//
// BUSINESS INTERPRETATION:
//   0-30  = Low risk
//   31-60 = Medium risk; monitor quarterly
//   61-100 = High risk; mitigation plan required
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Risk matrix (bubble chart) on Dashboard 2.
//   Table ranked by risk index for procurement review meetings.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[SC] Supplier Risk Index =
AVERAGEX(
    VALUES( dim_supplier[supplier_id] ),
    CALCULATE(
        SUMX(
            dim_supplier,
            ( dim_supplier[financial_risk_score]    * 0.40 )
            + ( dim_supplier[geopolitical_risk_score] * 0.30 )
            + ( dim_supplier[quality_risk_score]      * 0.20 )
            + ( dim_supplier[lead_time_risk_score]    * 0.10 )
        )
    )
)


// ============================================================
// SECTION 2: MANUFACTURING KPIs
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 11: Plant Utilization %
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   How much of a plant's planned production capacity was actually
//   used. Plant Utilization % = Actual Production / Planned Production * 100.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   Underutilization wastes fixed overhead. Overutilization
//   accelerates equipment wear and increases defect risk.
//   Automotive plants typically target 85-95% utilization.
//
// BUSINESS INTERPRETATION:
//   < 70%  = Severe underutilization; review scheduling or demand signals
//   70-85% = Below target; opportunity for optimization
//   85-95% = Optimal range
//   > 95%  = Overloaded; risk of quality degradation and maintenance deferral
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Gauge chart per plant on Dashboard 6 (Manufacturing).
//   Compare utilization vs defect rate to find the quality-capacity tradeoff.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[MFG] Plant Utilization % =
DIVIDE(
    SUM( fact_production[actual_units_produced] ),
    SUM( fact_production[planned_units] ),
    0
) * 100


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 12: Downtime %
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   Percentage of total available production hours lost to
//   unplanned downtime. Downtime % = Downtime Hours / Total Available Hours * 100.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   Unplanned downtime is the largest driver of production
//   variance. In automotive, 1 hour of line downtime can cost
//   $100,000+ in lost output. Tracking this drives proactive
//   maintenance scheduling.
//
// BUSINESS INTERPRETATION:
//   < 2%   = World class (TPM benchmark)
//   2-5%   = Acceptable; review failure mode patterns
//   > 5%   = Critical; immediate maintenance intervention
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Trend line on Dashboard 6.
//   Pareto chart of downtime reasons (equipment, material, staffing).
//   Alert when weekly downtime % exceeds 5%.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[MFG] Downtime % =
DIVIDE(
    SUM( fact_production[downtime_hours] ),
    SUM( fact_production[available_hours] ),
    0
) * 100


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 13: Defect Rate %
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   Percentage of produced units that fail quality inspection.
//   Defect Rate % = Defect Units / Total Units Produced * 100.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   Defects drive warranty costs, recall risk, and rework expense.
//   Automotive OEM quality targets are often expressed in PPM
//   (parts per million), but % is more intuitive for dashboards.
//
// BUSINESS INTERPRETATION:
//   < 0.1% = Excellent (< 1,000 PPM)
//   0.1-0.5% = Acceptable
//   > 0.5%   = Investigate; potential systemic quality issue
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Line chart over time on Dashboard 6.
//   Compare across plants and product lines.
//   Correlate with supplier changes or raw material batch changes.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[MFG] Defect Rate % =
DIVIDE(
    SUM( fact_production[defect_units] ),
    SUM( fact_production[actual_units_produced] ),
    0
) * 100


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 14: OEE (Overall Equipment Effectiveness)
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   OEE = Availability * Performance * Quality (all as decimals).
//   - Availability = (Available Hours - Downtime Hours) / Available Hours
//   - Performance  = Actual Output / (Ideal Cycle Time * Operating Time)
//                    Simplified here as Actual Units / Planned Units
//   - Quality      = Good Units / Total Units
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   OEE is the international standard for measuring manufacturing
//   productivity (ISO 22400). A world-class OEE is 85%. It exposes
//   the three largest sources of production loss simultaneously.
//
// BUSINESS INTERPRETATION:
//   > 85%  = World class
//   65-85% = Typical; identify dominant loss category
//   < 65%  = Significant losses; structured improvement needed
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Waterfall chart decomposing OEE into Availability, Performance,
//   and Quality losses on Dashboard 6.
//   Trend line for OEE by plant and shift.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[MFG] OEE % =
VAR Availability =
    DIVIDE(
        SUM( fact_production[available_hours] ) - SUM( fact_production[downtime_hours] ),
        SUM( fact_production[available_hours] ),
        0
    )
VAR Performance =
    DIVIDE(
        SUM( fact_production[actual_units_produced] ),
        SUM( fact_production[planned_units] ),
        0
    )
VAR Quality =
    DIVIDE(
        SUM( fact_production[actual_units_produced] ) - SUM( fact_production[defect_units] ),
        SUM( fact_production[actual_units_produced] ),
        0
    )
RETURN
    Availability * Performance * Quality * 100


// ============================================================
// SECTION 3: SALES KPIs
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 15: Forecast Accuracy %
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   How close demand forecasts were to actual sales.
//   Formula: (1 - ABS(Actual - Forecast) / Forecast) * 100
//   Clamped to 0% minimum (cannot be negative).
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   Poor forecast accuracy leads to either overstock (high holding
//   cost) or stockout (line stoppage). Improving forecast accuracy
//   by 5% can reduce inventory by 10-15% in automotive supply chains.
//
// BUSINESS INTERPRETATION:
//   > 90%  = Excellent
//   80-90% = Acceptable; review outliers
//   < 80%  = Forecasting model needs recalibration
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Trend line on Dashboard 5 (Demand & Sales).
//   Compare accuracy by vehicle model and region.
//   Identify months with systematic over/under-forecast.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[SLS] Forecast Accuracy % =
VAR ActualSales  = SUM( fact_sales[units_sold] )
VAR ForecastSales = SUM( fact_sales[units_forecasted] )
VAR AbsError     = ABS( ActualSales - ForecastSales )
RETURN
    MAX(
        0,
        ( 1 - DIVIDE( AbsError, ForecastSales, 0 ) ) * 100
    )


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 16: Revenue Growth %
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   Period-over-period revenue growth percentage.
//   (Current Revenue - Prior Period Revenue) / Prior Period Revenue * 100.
//   Prior period is determined by the date filter context using
//   DATEADD for month-over-month or year-over-year.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   Revenue growth validates whether the supply chain is scaling
//   to support business expansion. Negative growth signals demand
//   contraction requiring supply chain rightsizing.
//
// BUSINESS INTERPRETATION:
//   Context-dependent; compare against budget/target.
//   Use alongside cost KPIs to determine profitable growth.
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   KPI card with variance arrow on Dashboard 1 and Dashboard 5.
//   Swap prior period using a slicer (MoM / YoY / QoQ).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[SLS] Revenue Growth % YoY =
VAR CurrentRevenue =
    SUM( fact_sales[revenue] )
VAR PriorYearRevenue =
    CALCULATE(
        SUM( fact_sales[revenue] ),
        DATEADD( dim_date[date], -1, YEAR )
    )
RETURN
    DIVIDE(
        CurrentRevenue - PriorYearRevenue,
        PriorYearRevenue,
        BLANK()
    ) * 100


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 17: Regional Demand Growth
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   Year-over-year unit demand growth for the region(s) in the
//   current filter context. Expressed as a percentage change in
//   units_sold rather than revenue to strip out price effects.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   Regional growth rates inform safety stock levels, warehouse
//   capacity planning, and distribution network design. A growing
//   region may need a new distribution center; a shrinking one
//   may need rationalization.
//
// BUSINESS INTERPRETATION:
//   Compare against regional GDP and vehicle registration trends.
//   Regions growing > 10% YoY warrant capacity investment review.
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Choropleth map on Dashboard 5 shaded by demand growth %.
//   Rank regions in a bar chart for executive comparison.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[SLS] Regional Demand Growth % YoY =
VAR CurrentUnits =
    SUM( fact_sales[units_sold] )
VAR PriorYearUnits =
    CALCULATE(
        SUM( fact_sales[units_sold] ),
        DATEADD( dim_date[date], -1, YEAR )
    )
RETURN
    DIVIDE(
        CurrentUnits - PriorYearUnits,
        PriorYearUnits,
        BLANK()
    ) * 100


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 18: Revenue per Unit
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   Average selling price (revenue divided by units sold).
//   Useful for tracking pricing realization and mix effects.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   Revenue growth can be volume-driven or price-driven. Tracking
//   revenue per unit isolates pricing power and detects mix shift
//   toward lower-margin vehicle segments.
//
// BUSINESS INTERPRETATION:
//   Compare against standard cost to estimate gross margin per unit.
//   Declining revenue per unit may signal discounting or mix shift.
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Secondary Y-axis on revenue trend chart on Dashboard 5.
//   Break down by vehicle model / region.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[SLS] Revenue per Unit =
DIVIDE(
    SUM( fact_sales[revenue] ),
    SUM( fact_sales[units_sold] ),
    BLANK()
)


// ============================================================
// SECTION 4: TIME INTELLIGENCE MEASURES
// ============================================================
// NOTE: All time intelligence measures require a continuous,
// gap-free dim_date table marked as a Date Table in Power BI.
// See 02_power_bi_data_model.md for setup instructions.
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 19: Revenue MTD
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   Cumulative revenue from the first day of the current month
//   to the last date in the current filter context.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   MTD allows mid-month progress tracking against monthly
//   revenue targets, enabling early course correction.
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   KPI card on Dashboard 1 with a progress bar vs monthly budget.
//   Bullet chart comparing MTD vs MTD prior year.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[TI] Revenue MTD =
CALCULATE(
    SUM( fact_sales[revenue] ),
    DATESMTD( dim_date[date] )
)


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 20: Revenue YTD
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   Cumulative revenue from January 1 of the current year
//   to the last date in the current filter context.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   YTD tracks annual target attainment. Essential for executive
//   reporting and board-level performance discussions.
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Primary KPI card on Dashboard 1.
//   Annotate with annual target line on area chart.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[TI] Revenue YTD =
CALCULATE(
    SUM( fact_sales[revenue] ),
    DATESYTD( dim_date[date] )
)


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 21: Revenue vs Prior Year
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   The absolute dollar variance between current period revenue
//   and the same period in the prior year. Positive = growth.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   Absolute variance gives CFOs and executives the dollar
//   magnitude of growth/decline, complementing the % growth measure.
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Variance column in summary tables.
//   Waterfall chart showing year-over-year bridge on Dashboard 1.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[TI] Revenue vs Prior Year =
VAR CurrentRevenue = SUM( fact_sales[revenue] )
VAR PriorYearRevenue =
    CALCULATE(
        SUM( fact_sales[revenue] ),
        SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR( dim_date[date] )
    )
RETURN
    CurrentRevenue - PriorYearRevenue


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 22: On-Time Delivery % Rolling 12 Months
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   A 12-month trailing average of On-Time Delivery %, smoothing
//   seasonal spikes and showing the underlying performance trend.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   Point-in-time OTD can be noisy. A rolling 12-month view
//   reveals structural improvement or decline in supplier
//   performance that single-month views can obscure.
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Line chart on Dashboard 2 overlaid with monthly OTD bars.
//   Used in supplier scorecards to determine contract renewals.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[TI] On-Time Delivery % Rolling 12M =
CALCULATE(
    [SC] On-Time Delivery %,
    DATESINPERIOD(
        dim_date[date],
        LASTDATE( dim_date[date] ),
        -12,
        MONTH
    )
)


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 23: Inventory Value MoM Change
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   Month-over-month change in total inventory value (in currency).
//   Positive = inventory build-up; negative = drawdown.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   Rapid inventory build-up consumes working capital. A drawdown
//   may signal healthy sell-through or dangerous depletion.
//   Finance teams use this to forecast working capital requirements.
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Waterfall or bar chart on Dashboard 3.
//   Color: positive values in amber (inventory risk), negative in green.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[TI] Inventory Value MoM Change =
VAR CurrentInventory =
    SUM( fact_inventory[inventory_value] )
VAR PriorMonthInventory =
    CALCULATE(
        SUM( fact_inventory[inventory_value] ),
        DATEADD( dim_date[date], -1, MONTH )
    )
RETURN
    CurrentInventory - PriorMonthInventory


// ============================================================
// SECTION 5: CONDITIONAL / ALERT MEASURES
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 24: Supplier Risk Status
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   Returns a text label ("High Risk", "Medium Risk", "Low Risk")
//   based on the [SC] Supplier Risk Index score.
//   High:   Score > 60
//   Medium: Score 31-60
//   Low:    Score 0-30
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   Text labels enable conditional formatting icons and table
//   color coding in Power BI without requiring users to interpret
//   raw numbers.
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Status column in supplier table on Dashboard 2.
//   Use with Power BI conditional formatting to show red/amber/green.
//   Filter page to show only "High Risk" suppliers for action list.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[ALT] Supplier Risk Status =
VAR RiskScore = [SC] Supplier Risk Index
RETURN
    SWITCH(
        TRUE(),
        RiskScore > 60, "High Risk",
        RiskScore > 30, "Medium Risk",
        RiskScore >= 0, "Low Risk",
        "Unknown"
    )


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 25: Stockout Alert
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   Returns a severity label based on days of supply remaining
//   (quantity_on_hand / average_daily_demand).
//   CRITICAL: <= 3 days of supply
//   WARNING:  4-7 days of supply
//   OK:       > 7 days of supply
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   A simple "days of supply" threshold translates inventory
//   levels into actionable urgency for warehouse managers and
//   procurement teams without needing to understand raw stock numbers.
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Alert badge column in inventory table on Dashboard 3.
//   Filter to CRITICAL items for daily expediting review.
//   Push CRITICAL items to email alert via Power Automate.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[ALT] Stockout Alert =
VAR DaysOfSupply =
    DIVIDE(
        SUM( fact_inventory[quantity_on_hand] ),
        AVERAGE( fact_inventory[average_daily_demand] ),
        BLANK()
    )
RETURN
    SWITCH(
        TRUE(),
        ISBLANK( DaysOfSupply ),     "No Data",
        DaysOfSupply <= 3,           "CRITICAL",
        DaysOfSupply <= 7,           "WARNING",
                                     "OK"
    )


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURE 26: Delivery Performance Status
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CALCULATES:
//   Returns a text label for the current On-Time Delivery %:
//   "On Track"    : OTD >= 95%
//   "At Risk"     : OTD 90-94%
//   "Off Track"   : OTD < 90%
//
// WHY IT MATTERS:
//   Provides a quick health status for delivery performance that
//   can be used in slicer filters, conditional formatting, and
//   executive summary cards without number interpretation.
//
// HOW TO USE IN DASHBOARD:
//   Status icon (checkmark / warning / X) on Dashboard 1 KPI card.
//   Table column on Dashboard 4 (Logistics) by carrier/route.
//   Use as a page-level filter in bookmark navigation.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

[ALT] Delivery Performance Status =
VAR OTD = [SC] On-Time Delivery %
RETURN
    SWITCH(
        TRUE(),
        OTD >= 95, "On Track",
        OTD >= 90, "At Risk",
        OTD >= 0,  "Off Track",
        "No Data"
    )


// ============================================================
// END OF FILE: 01_supply_chain_kpi_measures.dax
// ============================================================
// QUICK REFERENCE INDEX:
//
// SUPPLY CHAIN KPIs:
//   [SC] On-Time Delivery %          (Measure 1)
//   [SC] Inventory Turnover          (Measure 2)
//   [SC] Fill Rate %                 (Measure 3)
//   [SC] Average Lead Time           (Measure 4)
//   [SC] Supplier Reliability Score  (Measure 5)
//   [SC] Transportation Cost Ratio   (Measure 6)
//   [SC] Perfect Order Rate          (Measure 7)
//   [SC] Stockout Rate               (Measure 8)
//   [SC] Days Inventory Outstanding  (Measure 9)
//   [SC] Supplier Risk Index         (Measure 10)
//
// MANUFACTURING KPIs:
//   [MFG] Plant Utilization %        (Measure 11)
//   [MFG] Downtime %                 (Measure 12)
//   [MFG] Defect Rate %              (Measure 13)
//   [MFG] OEE %                      (Measure 14)
//
// SALES KPIs:
//   [SLS] Forecast Accuracy %        (Measure 15)
//   [SLS] Revenue Growth % YoY       (Measure 16)
//   [SLS] Regional Demand Growth % YoY (Measure 17)
//   [SLS] Revenue per Unit           (Measure 18)
//
// TIME INTELLIGENCE:
//   [TI] Revenue MTD                           (Measure 19)
//   [TI] Revenue YTD                           (Measure 20)
//   [TI] Revenue vs Prior Year                 (Measure 21)
//   [TI] On-Time Delivery % Rolling 12M        (Measure 22)
//   [TI] Inventory Value MoM Change            (Measure 23)
//
// CONDITIONAL / ALERT:
//   [ALT] Supplier Risk Status         (Measure 24)
//   [ALT] Stockout Alert               (Measure 25)
//   [ALT] Delivery Performance Status  (Measure 26)
// ============================================================
